 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "NORTH: Cork and the road to Bantry. The road to the peninsula starts as a decent road and gets more honest as it goes."
  },
  {
   "x": 59,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "EAST: Kenmare and the Ring of Kerry. The Healy Pass goes over the top and comes down in Kerry, with its dignity mostly intact."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "THE HEALY PASS. A road that goes up a mountain the long way round, in hairpins. Gears, brakes and prayers are all recommended. It was built to give men work, and it gives everyone else a view."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "THE BEARA WAY. A walking route of about 200 kilometres round the peninsula, in Cork and in Kerry. You can do it in a fortnight. You can also do it in sections, with a pint at the end of each."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "healy_pass_allihies",
   "title": "The Allihies Copper Mines",
   "text": "Copper was mined at Allihies from 1812 until the 1880s. The mines belonged to the Puxley family of Dunboy Castle, and many of the miners were brought over from Cornwall, which is why the engine houses on the hill look like the ones in Cornwall. The Cornish went home or went to America when the copper ran out, and the Irish went to America and Cornwall and everywhere else. A place that looks this empty was once very busy."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "healy_pass_dursey",
   "title": "The Dursey Cable Car",
   "text": "A cable car carries a handful of people at a time across Dursey Sound to Dursey Island, a trip of a few minutes over a strait with a strong current. It is the only cable car in Ireland that crosses the sea. It has carried sheep and cattle as well as walkers, in the days when the island's farms were still working."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "healy_pass_bantry",
   "title": "The French at Bantry Bay",
   "text": "In December 1796 a French fleet sailed into Bantry Bay carrying an army of about fourteen thousand men, with Wolfe Tone among them, to help the United Irishmen. Storms scattered the ships and prevented a landing. Nobody who has stood in Bantry Bay in December will wonder why. The fleet turned for home, and a great deal of Irish history turned on the weather."
  }
 ],
